/-!
# Excluding larger-field triality parabolic residuals

The index-three two-core of the source residual makes that residual solvable,
bounds the three-rank of the local intersection by one, and forces three to
divide its order. Transport the specified component Sylow subgroup through
an actual isomorphism to the genuine D₄ graph-field carrier, then conjugate
it to the root unipotent subgroup.

The concrete Tits system shows that an overgroup of the root Borel with
solvable O^{3′} residual is the Borel: either nonempty simple-root index
would put an actual Levi SL₂(q) or SL₂(q³) into it. For q > 2 these Levi
groups are nonsolvable and generated by their three-elements. This uses
neither an auxiliary triality carrier nor a presumed parabolic catalogue.

The remaining Borel has torus GF(q³)ˣ × GF(q)ˣ. In even field degree its
two factors each contribute positive three-rank. In odd degree its order
q¹²(q³ - 1)(q - 1) is prime to three. Both cases contradict the source
residual constraints. All residuals use the prime-power-generated O^{3′}
convention; normality of this subgroup is intrinsic to its own group.

Source: Aschbacher--Smith, combined manuscript (1 March 2004),
15.3.43, printed p.1140; 1.1.5(3)(a), pp.501--502; and A.2.3--4, p.33.
The genuine root and Levi calculations are from GLS3 §§2.3--2.6.
-/

public section

